    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Current research on the three main fracture sets identified on Raplee monocline and the main fracture sets on the neighboring Comb monocline to the east is focused on determining the burial and exhumation history of those fractures and on investigating the subsurface fluids that used the interconnected fracture networks as conduits. Core samples of fractures have been collected from limestone and siltstone layers along Raplee and at one location on Comb. Thirty-one samples represent several fracture geometries of each systematic fracture set. Fracture geometries include single, linear fractures, fracture tips, abutment relationships, overlapping fractures, shear fractures, local shear zones, splay fractures, splay initiation and termination points, and a minor thrust fault. Observations of fracture geometries and fracture fill composition and structure from several fractures in thin section are described to support kinematic interpretations of fracture development. Microscopic and geochemical techniques are introduced to address the future direction of this research.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Seyum, Solomon and Pollard, David D. (2010). Diagenetic Mineral Precipitation in Fractures: Implications for the Tectonic History of Raplee and Comb Monoclines, Utah. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/bw184xc0241",
